{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially during uptrends/rallies. Apply an upward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when the market transitions from flat/down to a sustained rally, the model reacts too slowly and underestimates the slope. Add a positive trend term when recent returns turn positive and volatility rises.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: the error grows with forecast horizon (small early, larger mid-to-late window). Use a horizon-increasing uplift (e.g., ramping adjustment) rather than a constant offset.",
    "Underpricing of breakout regimes: after a local low and subsequent rebound, the model continues to forecast mean-reversion back toward prior levels, but the market stays in a higher regime. Detect breakouts (price above recent moving average/high) and shift forecasts to a higher regime.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth and miss the magnitude of moves in strong bullish periods. Increase amplitude (widen distribution / add volatility scaling) when recent realized volatility increases.",
    "Asymmetry: model handles declines better than rallies; largest errors occur on upside days. Use asymmetric corrections—stronger upward adjustments after positive momentum than downward adjustments after negative momentum."
  ]
}
